WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.759
Since he made his debut in Batman #1, the
Joker has been the Dark Knight’s deadliest

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:05.860
arch-nemesis.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.640
As the years went on, however, his crimes
went beyond thematic robberies and into brutal

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.860
murders, including clubbing Robin to death
with a crowbar.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.820
That raises a question that’s hard to ignore:
why doesn’t Batman just kill him?

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.300
“Come on, hit me!

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:23.520
Hit me!”

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:30.800
It’s pretty clear after all these decades
that putting a permanent end to the Joker

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:34.890
would solve at least half of Gotham City’s
problems, which makes it difficult for plenty

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.840
of readers to suspend their disbelief.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:40.720
If you really want to know the actual, definitive
answer, though, we’ve got it.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:41.960
You just might not like it.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.830
That's because the answer exists entirely
in our world, where Batman is a fictional

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:51.000
character designed to appear in movies, TV
shows, and at least four comic books every

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:51.940
month, forever,:

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:55.340
If Batman kills the Joker, we can't have any
more Joker stories.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.280
He is, in very blunt and realistic terms,
too popular to die.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.720
And he's been that way since the beginning.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:05.160
From the first day that Jerry Robinson drew
him on the page, the contrast of the smiling

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.660
murderer and the scowling, spooky hero made
him Batman’s perfect arch-enemy.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:14.880
Even by the dawn of the Silver Age, when Batman
became a much brighter, more superheroic character,

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.360
the Joker remained one of the most prominent
villains.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:20.160
All of which is to say that Joker stories
sell.

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:24.579
They sell a lot, and on top of that, he's
an interesting character that creators like

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.720
to make comics about as much as fans like
to read them..

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:32.380
Unfortunately, Batman’s real-world inability
to kill the Joker has to be justified in the

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:36.670
story, and that justification has become exponentially
more difficult over time.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:41.220
It’s very easy to understand why Batman
keeps taking the Joker back to Arkham Asylum

00:01:41.229 --> 00:01:45.289
when he's robbing banks and committing gimmick
crimes, even if there's an occasional murder

00:01:45.289 --> 00:01:46.720
thrown in to keep him scary.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:51.320
After all, no one ever asks why Batman doesn't
just drag the Riddler out back and shoot him.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:55.840
By the time we got to the '80s, though, as
Batman himself was redefined for a new era,

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.720
the Joker was forced to become every bit as
unstoppable, just on the other side.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:03.979
This was when he truly became Batman's opposite,
and if Batman was going to be the greatest

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:07.500
hero in the world, the Joker had to be the
absolute worst villain.

00:02:07.729 --> 00:02:12.040
Before the decade was out, the Joker went
from being an occasionally murderous arch-villain

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:17.400
to the psychopath who shot and assaulted Barbara
Gordon, tortured her father, killed Robin,

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.840
and tried to murder the entire United Nations.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:21.800
In fact, all that happened in one year.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:26.470
By the end of the '90s, the Joker was leaving
a body count in the double digits at minimum

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:31.300
every time he showed up, and was pretty consistently
characterized as an irredeemable monster.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:36.360
When Jason Todd, the former Robin that the
Joker crowbarred to death, came back to life

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:42.060
in 2005, he raised exactly these questions,
forcing Batman to provide an in-story reason

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.099
for why he can't ever kill his oldest, deadliest
enemy.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:49.700
In that story, Batman told Jason that if he
ever killed the Joker, he'd be unable to stop

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:54.060
himself from killing every other criminal
who got in his way, and Jason, like plenty

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:56.880
of readers, found that argument to be incredibly
frustrating.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:00.680
Can the world’s greatest detective really
not tell the difference between a guy who

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.900
turned an entire city into smiling zombies
and led a death parade through the streets,

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:05.400
from, like...

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:06.300
Kite-Man?

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:07.260
Really?

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:11.260
When you get right down to it, though, Batman’s
attitude toward killing the Joker actually

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:13.120
has an internal logic and consistency.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:14.930
It’s not actually about the Joker.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:16.420
It’s about Batman himself.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.980
While casual fans might attribute Batman’s
famous code against killing to the interference

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:26.370
of the comics code, it actually dates back
to 1941, predating the code by 13 years.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:30.900
Either way, it became a fundamental truth
about the character, reinforcing the idea

00:03:30.909 --> 00:03:35.090
that Batman has his roots in reacting to someone
taking a life - two lives, if you want to

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:36.020
be technical about it.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:39.760
That's the tragedy that he defines himself
in opposition to, and the thing that he's

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.530
always trying to prevent: death.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:45.820
He's a crimefighter, and while he might fight
bank robbers when they're around, the taking

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.620
of a life is the opposite of who he is.

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:51.800
Besides, don't forget that Batman lives in
a world where ghosts are 100 percent real.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:55.480
If you thought the Joker was trouble when
he was living, you absolutely do not want

00:03:55.489 --> 00:03:57.650
to know what happens once he shows up in the
afterlife.

